RULING OWED — where does a cross-package DEV helper live?

Five sites cannot be drained without deciding this, and it is a packaging decision on a
greenfield repo that has not published yet, which makes it the operator's:

  packages/forge/src/cli/commands/catalog.ts
  packages/forge/test/deploy/integrate-smoke.test.ts
  packages/forge/test/project/projection-facts.test.ts
  packages/forge/test/project/tree.test.ts
  packages/memory/test/cell-verb-roster.test.ts

THE BIND. `tooling/repo-root.ts` lives in canon. forge and memory cannot reach it:
  - no package edge, and creating one inverts the dependency direction (canon depends on
    forge, never the reverse);
  - a relative cross-package import breaks `typecheck:test` outright — measured, and
    already recorded in `event-vocabulary.test.ts`'s header as the reason that gate imports
    by package specifier instead.

THE OPTIONS, with what each costs:

  A. Move the helper into `@cratylus/runtime`, which every package already depends on.
     Cheapest to implement, and WRONG on its face: a repo-root finder is dev tooling and
     would ship to every consumer inside a published package.

  B. A private `@repo/tooling` workspace package with `"private": true`, which is the
     Turborepo/Nx convention for exactly this. Costs one package and one dependency edge
     per consumer; ships nothing. RECOMMENDED — it is the industry-standard shape and it
     keeps dev tooling out of the published surface, which W2 is about to freeze.

  C. Duplicate the helper per package. Rejected on sight: this corpus deletes duplicates.

  D. Leave the five pinned. Rejected: the gate's own doc commits the ratchet to reaching ∅
     and then being deleted, and a permanent five-entry exemption makes that a lie.

RECOMMENDATION: B. It should land BEFORE the publish pipeline, because adding a private
workspace package after the first release changes the dependency graph of published
manifests.
